WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.243
Kubernetes deployment scripts are often
created because teams need repeatable ways

2
00:00:08.323 --> 00:00:13.377
to apply manifests, update images, render
Helm charts,

3
00:00:13.457 --> 00:00:21.092
run Kustomize overlays, trigger rollouts,
check status, and coordinate releases.

4
00:00:21.172 --> 00:00:27.732
A script may look like a thin wrapper
around kubectl or a deployment tool,

5
00:00:27.812 --> 00:00:31.684
but it can still change real cluster
state.

6
00:00:31.764 --> 00:00:38.539
That makes it production automation, not
just command-line convenience.

7
00:00:38.619 --> 00:00:42.813
The impact of a deployment script can be
broad.

8
00:00:42.893 --> 00:00:50.098
It may update workloads, services, ingress
configuration, network exposure,

9
00:00:50.178 --> 00:00:57.168
volumes, service accounts, ConfigMaps,
Secrets, resource limits, probes,

10
00:00:57.248 --> 00:00:59.829
and availability behavior.

11
00:00:59.909 --> 00:01:03.995
A small script can determine what image
runs,

12
00:01:04.075 --> 00:01:10.097
which namespace receives a change, which
service account is used,

13
00:01:10.177 --> 00:01:14.479
and whether a release becomes visible to
users.

14
00:01:14.559 --> 00:01:18.861
Those are security and reliability
decisions.

15
00:01:18.941 --> 00:01:24.963
Common deployment incidents often begin
with ordinary mistakes:

16
00:01:25.043 --> 00:01:31.710
the wrong cluster context, the wrong
namespace, an unexpected image tag,

17
00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:37.060
an overbroad identity, an unreviewed
rendered manifest,

18
00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.936
or an ignored command failure.

19
00:01:40.016 --> 00:01:43.134
The risk is not that teams use Bash.

20
00:01:43.214 --> 00:01:50.527
The risk is that a simple wrapper can hide
assumptions that should be explicit and

21
00:01:50.607 --> 00:01:54.801
reviewable before production state
changes.

22
00:01:54.881 --> 00:02:02.301
Secure Kubernetes deployment scripting
treats every deployment as a controlled

23
00:02:02.381 --> 00:02:04.532
change to a real system.

24
00:02:04.612 --> 00:02:12.140
The defensive goal is scoped, reviewed,
observable, and recoverable automation.

25
00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:17.704
The script should validate its target, use
least privilege,

26
00:02:17.784 --> 00:02:22.301
protect credentials, invoke tools
predictably,

27
00:02:22.381 --> 00:02:28.080
expose enough information for review,
verify rollout health,

28
00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:33.860
and support recovery when a change does
not behave as expected.
